# .servicepackupdateignore
# Files and patterns to exclude when updating servicepack framework
# Uses rsync exclude syntax - supports wildcards and paths

# Example: Custom framework modifications
# Makefile
# Dockerfile.dev
# .golangci.yml

# Example: Custom scripts
# scripts/custom_*
# scripts/deploy.sh

# Example: Local configuration files
# *.local
# .env*
# config/local.yml

# User files - never overwritten by framework updates
cmd/init.go
cmd/commands.go

# Dependabot config - your own dependency policy (which deps, the cooldown
# window, the own-package excludes). Ignored by default so a framework update
# never clobbers your tweaks. Delete this line if you'd rather the framework
# keep pushing its common dependabot.yml to every servicepack-based project.
.github/dependabot.yml

# --- The framework's own repo furniture --------------------------------------
#
# The update is an rsync of the framework tree over yours, so anything shipped
# here that isn't a baseline for YOUR app doesn't get "updated" - it gets ADDED.
# The entries below exist to publish, fund and document servicepack itself.
# They are wrong on arrival for a downstream rather than a starting point you
# customize, which is why they're ignored by default instead of left for every
# project to discover on its own.

# Mirrors this repo to GitLab and Codeberg and saves it to the Wayback Machine.
# servicepack is public; your project may not be. On a private repo this turns
# your next tag into a public disclosure, and the archive does not forget.
# Delete this line only if your repo is public AND you want those mirrors.
.github/workflows/mirror-and-archive.yml

# Relays issues back from those mirrors. Without them it is a cron job that
# runs forever and finds nothing.
.github/workflows/issue-pull.yml

# The framework's pipeline builds and releases THE FRAMEWORK - including
# publishing its agent skill to ClawHub, which your project doesn't have. Your
# pipeline is yours: codegen gates, web builds, integration suites, image
# publishing. Delete this line if you want the framework's baseline pipeline
# pushed over yours on every update.
.github/workflows/pipeline.yml

# Sponsor links for servicepack's author, not yours.
.github/FUNDING.yml

# The agent skill describing SERVICEPACK. Landing it in your repo tells any
# agent reading it that it is looking at the framework rather than your app.
.agents

# Secret-scanning allowlist. Allowlists are per-repo by nature - they name the
# untracked paths THIS tree has - so the framework's would silence the wrong
# things in yours, or drown you in findings from paths you don't have.
.gitleaks.toml

# Docker build context. Pairs with Dockerfile / Dockerfile.dev, which are
# already yours - your context excludes whatever your app builds (a web tree,
# fixtures) and the framework has no concept of those.
.dockerignore

# Note: internal/pkg/services/* is always excluded - your services are never touched